r","feedback_revision":"1edd1bfb4a0d8d978b9fd0c5"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Are the sets $\\{1,\\,2\\}$ and $\\{3,\\,4\\}$ disjoint?","options":["Yes, because their union is $\\varnothing$.","Yes, because their intersection is $\\varnothing$.","No, because they both have two elements.","No, because neither set is empty."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a082ed5b77664065ffbb57d4"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For any set $A$, which statement about $A$ and $\\varnothing$ is correct?","options":["They are equal.","They have intersection $A$.","They are disjoint.","They are disjoint only when $A$ is finite."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a368f3d8909722bec395a93d"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does disjointness express mutual exclusivity in set theory?","options":["Because both sets must be empty.","Because every element belongs to both sets.","Because membership in both sets is impossible.","Because their union has exactly one element."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2db326c0be1217d6206feaef"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What must hold for two sets $A$ and $B$ to be disjoint?","options":["$A = B$","$A\\cap B = \\varnothing$","$A\\cup B = \\varnothing$","$A\\subseteq B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e1f0ff26d7aa21233fe1e531"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A = \\{1,\\,2,\\,3\\}$ and $B = \\{3,\\,4\\}$. Are $A$ and $B$ disjoint?","options":["Yes, because $A\\cup B$ is non-empty.","No, because $3\\in A\\cap B$.","Yes, because $A$ has more elements than $B$.","No, because both sets contain $4$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"568ec632d7f8f9beb7133f51"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A = \\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}:x \\lt 0\\}$ and $B = \\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}:x \\gt 0\\}$. Which statement is correct?","options":["$A$ and $B$ are disjoint.","$A$ and $B$ have intersection $\\{0\\}$.","$A$ and $B$ are equal.","$A$ and $B$ are not disjoint because both are infinite."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a3d09c5e8eaecad0d6778461"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement shows why disjoint sets need not be distinct?","options":["$A\\cap B = \\varnothing$ always implies $A = B$.","$A\\cup B = \\varnothing$ while $A\\ \\neq B$.","$A\\cap A = A$ for every non-empty set $A$.","$\\varnothing\\cap\\varnothing = \\varnothing$ while $\\varnothing = \\varnothing$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7c2dbf206e63417e7936eaa8"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If a set $A$ is disjoint from itself, what can be concluded about $A$?","options":["$A$ has exactly one element.","$A = \\varnothing$","$A$ is any finite set.","$A$ must be equal to $\\mathbb{R}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3d1eadcc83ed9d679e28f040"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $A\\cap B = \\varnothing$ and $A = B$. What must hold?","options":["$A$ and $B$ each have one element.","$A = B = \\varnothing$","$A$ and $B$ must each be infinite.","$A$ and $B$ are non-empty distinct sets."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b2c1c5df683a36d3e9c17578"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_disjoint_sets_0e507855_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A = \\{1,\\,2\\}$, $B = \\{2,\\,3\\}$, and $C = \\{4\\}$. Which pair is disjoint?","options":["$A$ and $B$","$B$ and $B$","$A$ and $C$","$A$ and $A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5b98be7d4fdfa714ea3883c7"},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_disjoint_sets_new_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What must be true of common elements of disjoint sets?","options":["There are no common elements.","There is exactly one common element.","Every element is common.","Only rational numbers can be common."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6bc79e395694e66cdea8b3ae"},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_disjoint_sets_new_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which equation defines disjointness of $A$ and $B$?","options":["$A\\cap B=\\varnothing$","$A\\cup B=\\varnothing$","$A=B$","$A\\setminus B=\\varnothing$ always"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0aad57683adccb8dec4d11df"},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_disjoint_sets_new_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $A$ disjoint from $\\varnothing$ for any set $A$?","options":["Every set equals the empty set, universally and without exception.","Their union is always empty.","$A$ must be finite.","The empty set has no elements to share with $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"81c8706e7fca69c0b31c4fa4"},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_disjoint_sets_new_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Are $\\{1,3\\}$ and $\\{3,5\\}$ disjoint?","options":["No, because they share $3$.","Yes, because both sets have two elements.","Yes, because their union is non-empty.","No, because they do not share $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"275f8d94d90655bd89957241"},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_disjoint_sets_new_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A$ be the even integers and $B$ be the odd integers. Are $A$ and $B$ disjoint?","options":["No, every integer is both even and odd.","Yes, no integer is both even and odd.","No, because both sets are infinite.","Yes, only if both sets are empty."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8fb6923a3fe5f80104710974"},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_disjoint_sets_new_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A\\cap B=\\varnothing$, which statement follows?","options":["$A$ and $B$ are disjoint.","$A$ and $B$ must be equal and non-empty.","$A\\cup B=\\varnothing$ necessarily.","$A\\subseteq B$ necessarily."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ffedf72c10a27f1f27927b60"},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_disjoint_sets_new_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can disjointness simplify counting arguments?","options":["Every element is counted twice.","The sets must be equal.","No element is counted in both sets.","The union must be empty."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2649d7e106426e8e38e8087a"},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_disjoint_sets_new_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which pair is disjoint?","options":["$\\{a\\}$ and $\\{b,c\\}$","$\\{a,b\\}$ and $\\{b,c\\}$","$\\{c\\}$ and $\\{a,c\\}$","$\\{a,b\\}$ and $\\{a\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"66a51df3111eafddccc254bf"}]}}